## Why This Is Asked
Observability is essential for diagnosing issues early; beginners learn instrumenting apps, metrics, and dashboards using Azure Monitor.

## Key Concepts
- Application Insights integration with Azure Functions
- Custom metrics and dependency tracking
- Azure Monitor dashboards and alert rules
- CI/CD wiring for instrumentation
- Cost/retention trade-offs of telemetry data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example instrumentation snippet for App Insights
const appInsights = require('applicationinsights');
appInsights.setup(process.env.APPINSIGHTS_INSTRUMENTATIONKEY).start();
const client = appInsights.defaultClient;
exports.handler = async function(context, req) {
  const start = Date.now();
  // ... business logic
  const latency = Date.now() - start;
  client.trackMetric({name: 'requestLatencyMs', value: latency});
  try {
    // ... do work
  } catch (e) {
    client.trackException({exception: e});
  }
  client.trackMetric({name: 'appErrorCount', value: 0});
  context.res = { status: 200, body: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust sampling to manage costs while preserving useful signals?
- How would you visualize SQL query latency and failed dependencies in the dashboard?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical multi-tenant governance, cross-subscription access, and automation in a real-world analytics scenario. It tests data isolation, cost control, and operational readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Data Lake Gen2, Azure Synapse Spark
- Azure Lighthouse for cross-tenant access
- API Management as gateway
- Azure Policy, RBAC, tagging, encryption
- Terraform + GitHub Actions CI/CD, Cost Management budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: create Lighthouse assignment (illustrative)
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { LighthouseClient } = require('@azure/arm-lighthouse');
async function createAssignment() {
  const cred = new DefaultAzureCredential();
  const client = new LighthouseClient(cred, '<subscription-id>');
  await client.assignments.createOrUpdate('<scope>', '<assignment-name>', {
    properties: {
      principalId: '<principal-id>',
      roleDefinitionId: '/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions/...'
    }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-tenant access boundaries and revoke access when needed?
- What monitoring and alerting would you implement to catch misconfigurations early?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of identity-based security, secret management, and infrastructure as code in a beginner Azure context, with emphasis on environment isolation, governance, and CI/CD integration.

## Key Concepts
- Managed identities and Key Vault access policies
- Environment isolation with separate Key Vaults
- Tagging governance and policy enforcement
- Bicep/ARM IaC deployment and GitHub Actions

## Code Example

```bicep
// Minimal example showing a Function App identity and Key Vault access
resource kv 'Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2021-04-01-preview' = {
  name: 'kv-${environment}'
  location: resourceGroup.location
  properties: {
    tenantId: subscription().tenantId
    sku: {
      family: 'A'
      name: 'standard'
    }
    accessPolicies: [
      {
        tenantId: subscription().tenantId
        objectId: functionIdentity.properties.principalId
        permissions: {
          secrets: [
            'get'
            'list'
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
    tags: {
      Environment: environment
      Project: projectName
      Owner: ownerTag
    }
  }
}

resource functionIdentity 'Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities@2023-01-31' = {
  name: 'func-identity-${environment}'
  location: resourceGroup.location
  tags: {
    Environment: environment
    Project: projectName
  }
}

resource functionApp 'Microsoft.Web/sites@2022-03-01' = {
  name: 'func-${environment}'
  location: resourceGroup.location
  kind: 'functionapp'
  identity: {
    type: 'UserAssigned'
    userAssignedIdentities: {
      '${functionIdentity.id}': {}
    }
  }
  properties: {
    serverFarmId: appServicePlan.id
    siteConfig: {
      appSettings: [
        {
          name: 'KEY_VAULT_NAME'
          value: kv.name
        }
      ]
    }
  }
  tags: {
    Environment: environment
    Project: projectName
  }
}
```

## GitHub Actions Workflow

```yaml
name: Deploy Azure Resources

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

env:
  ENVIRONMENT: ${{ github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' && 'prod' || 'dev' }}

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      id-token: write
      contents: read
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Azure Login
        uses: azure/login@v1
        with:
          client-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}
          tenant-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}
          subscription-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}

      - name: Deploy Bicep
        uses: azure/arm-deploy@v1
        with:
          resourceGroupName: rg-${{ env.ENVIRONMENT }}
          template: main.bicep
          parameters: environment=${{ env.ENVIRONMENT }} projectName=myapp ownerTag=team-email

      - name: Deploy Function Code
        uses: Azure/functions-action@v1
        with:
          app-name: func-${{ env.ENVIRONMENT }}
          package: function-app/
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to extend a simple Azure Fundamentals stack with real feature-flag patterns, connecting front-end and back-end behavior to centralized configuration while maintaining security best practices.

## Key Concepts

- Azure App Configuration service and feature flags
- Front-end integration with App Configuration JavaScript SDK
- Server-side access via Azure AD managed identity
- GitHub Actions for infrastructure and configuration deployment
- Latency, caching, and cost optimization considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
